--TEST--
One declaration decides both of `Core\Json`'s directions, and the half of that still asked at run time is the encoder — `rule:core-classes/derive-attribute`, `rule:core-classes/derive-generates-what-is-missing`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:core-classes/derive-attribute`: a class participates in a wire format
// by carrying the attribute. These three declare the same two fields; only the
// first line of the first one differs, which is what makes the pair a bound
// rather than two examples.
#[Core\Json\Derive]
final class Row {
    public int $n;
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(int $n, string $name) {
        $this->n = $n;
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

final class Plain {
    public int $n;
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(int $n, string $name) {
        $this->n = $n;
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

final class Legacy {
    public int $n;
    public string $name;

    public function constructor(int $n, string $name) {
        $this->n = $n;
        $this->name = $name;
    }
}

// The declared class, both directions, and it is the constructor that built the
// decoded one.
var $row = Core\Json::decodeAs<Row>("{\"n\":1,\"name\":\"ada\"}");
echo "row-read=", $row->name, "\n";
bool $rowEncodes = true;
try {
    echo "row-wrote=", Core\Json::encode(new Row(1, "ada")), "\n";
} catch (LogicError $rowNoEncode) {
    $rowEncodes = false;
}

// The undeclared pair, at the door that still asks when it runs. The decoding
// direction names its class in the call, so
// `rule:core-classes/derive-attribute` has that one refused while compiling and
// `reject/a-class-that-declared-no-json-codec-is-refused-at-the-decode-site`
// pins it; encoding names no class anywhere but in the value it was handed, so
// it is answered here. The two cases together are the property that one
// attribute decides both directions: each door refuses exactly `Plain` and
// `Legacy`.
bool $plainEncodes = true;
try {
    var $plainWritten = Core\Json::encode(new Plain(1, "ada"));
    echo "plain-wrote=", $plainWritten, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $plainNoEncode) {
    $plainEncodes = false;
}
bool $legacyEncodes = true;
try {
    var $legacyWritten = Core\Json::encode(new Legacy(1, "ada"));
    echo "legacy-wrote=", $legacyWritten, "\n";
} catch (LogicError $legacyNoEncode) {
    $legacyEncodes = false;
}

// Asserted by counting rather than read off a line above: an encoder that grew
// a participation rule of its own — writing any class whose fields happen to be
// scalars — still prints plausibly per class and fails this.
int $wrote = 0;
if ($rowEncodes) {
    $wrote = $wrote + 1;
}
if ($plainEncodes) {
    $wrote = $wrote + 1;
}
if ($legacyEncodes) {
    $wrote = $wrote + 1;
}
echo "wrote=", $wrote, " of 3\n";

// § 10's two branches: the class was written by the program, so one it cannot
// encode is a bug in the program rather than something the world did, and the
// refusal is a `LogicError`.
try {
    var $unwritable = Core\Json::encode(new Plain(1, "ada"));
    echo "not refused\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $runtime) {
    echo "class=runtime\n";
} catch (LogicError $logic) {
    echo "class=logic\n";
}

// The class is answered before the document is, and for a class that can
// participate that leaves the syntax to fail on: an unreadable text under a
// declared class is the document's own error rather than the program's.
try {
    var $neverRow = Core\Json::decodeAs<Row>("{oops}");
    echo "not refused\n";
} catch (ParseError $parseRow) {
    echo "malformed-row=parse\n";
} catch (LogicError $logicRow) {
    echo "malformed-row=logic\n";
}
--EXPECT--
row-read=ada
row-wrote={"n":1,"name":"ada"}
wrote=1 of 3
class=logic
malformed-row=parse
